Dear Toni,

sorry for the delay, I'm on leave this week.

Let me address your questions in different messages.

For the first one, are you including the family effect (using the random component) in addition to the genetic effect? In that case, that is very likely the cause of the non-convergence. Indeed, the genetic and family effects are both trying to explain the same thing. The model is thus not identifiable.

If, instead, you are replacing the genetic effect by the family effect, then there could be a number of causes for the convergence problem. Perhaps many families with only one member or even no member at all? I would need to check the data to be sure.

About averaging the individual blups as a mean to obtain family effects and the bootstrap the estimates... yeah, in principle that would be ok. But resampling is delicate as you want to make sure to keep the number of observations in each family constant over replicates, and you need several observations per family to avoid resampling the same individual too much and biasing the estimation of the within-family variance down. If you don't need individual blups, I would rather estimate family effects directly.

Hope it helps.

ƒacu.-

On 31/10/2022 09:59, 'Toni K Clarke' via breedR wrote:

> Hi,
>
> I'm trying to calculate family blups for several different traits in 1440 full-sibs comprising 48 families (33 dams and 19 sires). I've used the following animal model with spatial covariates.
>
>   res = breedR::remlf90(
>     fixed = trait ~1,
>    genetic = list(model = "add_animal", pedigree = ped, id = "self"),
>        spatial = list(model = 'AR',
>                   coord = x[, c('plot_x','plot_y')]),
>    method="ai",data = x)
>
> If I create a family ID (dam:sire) and fit this as a random effect (random=~family) then the model tends not to converge or there is almost no variance associated with family (i presume all accounted for by the genetic effect). Is it OK to just average the individual blups for each family and perform some bootstrapping to get CI's rather than formally fitting family in the model?
>
> Also, I've been using the multi-trait model to estimate heritabilities and they are predictably higher. I'd like to include spatial covariates in this model but they take too long to run and often don't converge. I saw in one of your previous papers you had run single trait models to estimate the spatial effects and then adjusted your phenotypes before running a multi-trait model. Was this just a case of subtracting the spatial blups from the phenotype of interest before estimating heritability?
>
> Many Thanks
> Toni
>

